## H. W2 STATUS — HONEST REPORT (NOT a clean closure)

The role's claim "the event4 evidence finally closed" is REFUTED by the
machine-state evidence above. Two sub-claims were made for W2; one is
closed, one is NOT:

  ✅ CLOSED — uaccess rule ordering.
     The 69- rename does place the uaccess tag before 70-uaccess.rules /
     73-seat-late.rules, and the uaccess builtin is scheduled to run on
     event7 (udevadm test shows `RUN{builtin} : uaccess`). The
     pre-W2-double-tagging condition (99- rule firing after 70-) is
     removed: the stale 99- rule is gone, only the 69- rule ships.

  ❌ NOT CLOSED — ACL materialization on the Touch Bar input node.
     `getfacl /dev/input/event7` shows `user::rw-` only — NO
     `user:emzmo:rw-` entry. The event4 evidence (no user ACL on the
     Touch Bar input device) is REPRODUCED, not closed.

     ROOT CAUSE (machine-state backed): the `uaccess` builtin sets a
     user ACL for the active session's user ON THE DEVICE'S SEAT. The
     69- rule sets `ENV{ID_SEAT}="seat-touchbar"`. There is no
     `seat-touchbar` in logind (loginctl: "No seat 'seat-touchbar'
     known"; only `seat0` exists). With no active session on
     seat-touchbar, logind has no user to materialize an ACL for, so
     none is set. The DRM cards on seat0 get the ACL because the active
     user IS on seat0.

     The 69- rule's two goals CONFLICT on this machine:
       - goal 1 (libinput seat isolation): ENV{ID_SEAT}=seat-touchbar
         → moves the device OFF seat0, away from the active user.
       - goal 2 (uaccess ACL for the user): TAG+=uaccess
         → needs the device ON a seat with the active user.
     Setting ID_SEAT=seat-touchbar defeats the uaccess ACL. The 99-→69-
     rename fixed the *ordering* half of W2 but not the *materialization*
     half — because the materialization half was never an ordering bug,
     it is a seat-isolation vs. uaccess conflict.

     PRACTICAL IMPACT today: NONE. The Touch Bar input device is
     group-owned by `input` (`group::rw-`), and the active user emzmo
     is a member of the `input` group (the long-standing bench lane),
     so the user CAN open the device regardless of the missing ACL.
     This is the same "masked on the bench by the input group" condition
     documented in docs/test-logs/008/. The W2 finding's severity is
     therefore NOT escalated — but the closure claim is dropped.

     FOLLOW-UP (NOT this session — FREEZE RULE): an ADR is needed to
     reconcile the seat-isolation goal (ADR-009: libinput seat-touchbar
     context enumerates only the Touch Bar digitizer) with the uaccess
     goal (ADR-019: active-user ACL on the Touch Bar input node).
     Candidate resolutions to evaluate on a branch:
       (a) drop ENV{ID_SEAT}=seat-touchbar from the udev rule and rely
           on the daemon's libinput context filtering by VID:PID alone
           (loses seat isolation, simplest);
       (b) keep ID_SEAT=seat-touchbar and create/activate that seat in
           logind so uaccess has a user to materialize for (complex,
           may need a session on seat-touchbar);
       (c) keep ID_SEAT=seat-touchbar and grant group `input` on the
           device explicitly (the current de-facto state — make it
           intentional).
     The daemon's libinput seat-touchbar context (ADR-004/009) does not
     require a logind seat named `seat-touchbar` to exist — it just
     opens devices whose ID_SEAT matches. So (a) is a real behavioural
     change; (b)/(c) preserve the seat-filter behaviour. Decision
     deferred to an ADR on a branch.

This is filed as a NEW FINDING (NF-020-A): "uaccess ACL does not
materialize on the Touch Bar input device because ID_SEAT=seat-touchbar
moves it off the active session's seat." Severity: low (input group
masks it on the bench). Blocks the W2 "event4 evidence closed" claim.